  images: [{ file: "Port-Royal Habitation Nova Scotia", caption: "The reconstructed Habitation at Port-Royal" }, { file: "Red Bay Labrador Basque whaling", caption: "Basque roof tiles at Red Bay" }, { file: "Beothuk Shanawdithit", caption: "The taking of Demasduit, 1819" }, { file: "Nain Labrador Moravian", caption: "The Moravian church at Nain" }, { file: "Grand-Pré Acadian", caption: "Grand-Pré" }],
  narrativeContent: `The Gulf of St. Lawrence is a sea with three doors: the Strait of Belle Isle in the north, the Cabot Strait in the south and the narrow Strait of Canso. Around it live the Mi'kmaq of the Maritimes and the Gaspé, the Wolastoqiyik of the Saint John, the Innu of the north shore and Labrador and, farther north, the Inuit; on the island of Newfoundland lived the Beothuk, who painted themselves with red ochre and whom the English called Red Indians. Europeans reached these coasts before any other part of what is now Canada, and for a century they came to fish and went home.

## The fishery comes first

John Cabot's landfall of 24 June 1497 told Bristol that the sea off the new land was thick with cod. Within a generation Basque, Breton, Norman, Portuguese and English ships were drying fish on the shores every summer, and Basque whalers were rendering oil at Red Bay in the Strait of Belle Isle by the 1530s. Gaspar Corte-Real took captives from the coast in 1501 and was lost; Verrazzano followed the coast from Florida to Newfoundland in 1524; Cartier came through Belle Isle in 1534 and claimed the Gulf for France; Roberval's pilot Jean Alfonse went up the Labrador coast in 1542. None of them stayed. Sir Humphrey Gilbert took possession of Newfoundland at St. John's in 1583 and drowned on the way home.

## Colonies on a fishing coast

The first colonies were planted by fur and fish companies. De Monts and Champlain wintered at Sainte-Croix in 1604 and moved to Port-Royal on the Annapolis Basin in 1605; Lescarbot's Théâtre de Neptune and the Order of Good Cheer made the winter of 1606-07 the happiest in the colony's story, and the Mi'kmaq grand chief Membertou was baptized there in 1610. John Guy's Bristol colonists landed at Cuper's Cove in Conception Bay in 1610, and in 1612 Guy met the Beothuk at Bull Arm and shared a meal with them. Richard Whitbourne held the first court in Newfoundland at Trinity in 1615; David Kirke, who had taken Quebec, governed from Ferryland after 1638. Samuel Argall of Virginia burned Port-Royal in 1613, the first blow in a war over Acadia that ended only in 1763.

## Acadia

Isaac de Razilly brought settlers in 1632, and Charles de La Tour built his fort at the mouth of the Saint John in 1631 and fought his rival d'Aulnay for Acadia through the 1640s; La Tour's wife held the fort and lost it in 1645. Nicolas Denys bought the whole Gulf coast in 1653, built posts at Saint-Pierre on the Bras d'Or Lake and at Nipisiguit, and wrote the book that describes the Mi'kmaq and the fishery of his day. The Acadians who farmed the dyked marshes of Port-Royal, Grand-Pré and Beaubassin were deported by the British in 1755, after Fort Beauséjour fell.

## The Beothuk

On Newfoundland the story went another way. The Beothuk kept to themselves, taking iron from the empty fishing stations; the settlers shot them. By 1800 a few hundred were left in the Exploits valley and at Red Indian Lake. David Buchan's naval party went up the frozen river to make peace in January 1811 and lost two marines. In March 1819 John Peyton's party took Demasduit on the ice of the lake and killed her husband Nonosabasut; she died in January 1820. Her niece Shawnadithit, taken in 1823, lived five years with the Peytons and a year with William Cormack, drawing the maps and scenes that are most of what is known of her people, and died in St. John's on 6 June 1829, the last known Beothuk.

## Labrador

Labrador's Inuit met Europeans as enemies and traders along the Strait of Belle Isle; Louis Fornel of Quebec claimed Hamilton Inlet in 1743 and left men at North West River. Governor Palliser met five hundred Inuit at Chateau Bay in 1765. Mikak, kidnapped in 1767 and taken to London, helped Jens Haven's Moravians found Nain in 1771; her husband Tuglavina, the angakok, traded south against their wishes. George Cartwright fished and trapped at Sandwich Bay from 1775 and kept his journal. The Hudson's Bay Company came to Rigolet in 1836, and in 1839 John McLean and Erland Erlandson saw the Grand Falls of the Grand River. Mina Hubbard mapped the Naskaupi and George rivers in 1905. Nunatsiavut, the Labrador Inuit government, was established in 2005.

## The surveyors

James Cook learned his trade charting Newfoundland in 1763-67; Joseph DesBarres charted Nova Scotia and published the Atlantic Neptune; Henry Bayfield spent thirty years on the Gulf and river from 1827, and his Sailing Directions guided ships for a century. Joseph Jukes made the first geological survey of Newfoundland in 1839-40 and missed the iron of Bell Island.`,
